## PROVISIONAL — the upstream licence is disputed and this choice can change

The UYPYDJ deposit states two different licences, and this repository follows
the one in the deposit's own readme.

    readme inside the deposit, line 47   "Licenses/restrictions: CC BY 4.0"
    Borealis dataset record (V2)         CC BY-SA 4.0

Author's decision, 2026-09-01: proceed on CC BY 4.0 pending the depositor's
answer. The readme is the depositors' own statement, it sits inside the
deposit, and the same file certifies the data is "free of licensing and
intellectual property issues".

**This is provisional and reversible.** If the depositor confirms the Borealis
record governs, this file becomes CC BY-SA 4.0 and everything below applies:
ShareAlike propagates to adapted material, and what is redistributed here is
substantial — analysis/uypydj_hppc_resistance.csv is 57,674 rows,
uypydj_ecm.csv 27,894, uypydj_ocv.csv 11,703, plus 112 model files trained on
the dataset. Under CC BY-SA 4.0 section 4(b) a database built from a
substantial portion of the licensed contents is itself Adapted Material.

That would also reopen the publishing question: ShareAlike asks for adapted
material under CC BY-SA 4.0, and Elsevier offers CC BY 4.0, CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 or
its own subscription terms — none of them BY-SA. On the current CC BY 4.0
reading there is no conflict, which is part of why the answer matters.

